To address the interviewer's comment, I will demonstrate how a **secondary index** (using a `HashMap` for $O(1)$ lookups) significantly optimizes query performance in a collection of objects that would otherwise require $O(n)$ linear scans.

### Approach
In a standard list-based search, finding an item by a non-primary attribute is $O(n)$. By maintaining a `Map<K, List<V>>` (the secondary index) alongside our primary data structure, we trade memory space for query speed, reducing search time complexity to $O(1)$ (or $O(k)$ if multiple items share the same index key). I will implement a `StudentRepository` that indexes students by `department` to showcase this pattern.

### Implementation

```java
import java.util.*;

public class StudentRepository {
    // Primary storage
    private final List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

    // Secondary Index: Maps department name to a list of students in that department
    private final Map<String, List<Student>> deptIndex = new HashMap<>();

    public void addStudent(Student student) {
        students.add(student);
        // Update the secondary index
        deptIndex.computeIfAbsent(student.getDepartment(), k -> new ArrayList<>())
                 .add(student);
    }

    // Query using the secondary index - O(1) average time complexity
    public List<Student> getStudentsByDepartment(String dept) {
        return deptIndex.getOrDefault(dept, Collections.emptyList());
    }

    public static class Student {
        private final String id;
        private final String name;
        private final String department;

        public Student(String id, String name, String department) {
            this.id = id;
            this.name = name;
            this.department = department;
        }

        public String getDepartment() { return department; }
    }
}
```

### Why this works
1.  **Time Complexity:** Without the index, finding all students in a department requires iterating through the entire list ($O(n)$). With the `HashMap` index, the lookup is $O(1)$ on average, making the retrieval performance independent of the total number of students in the repository.
2.  **Trade-offs:** We incur a slight cost in **write time** and **memory usage**. Every time a student is added, we must update the map. However, in read-heavy systems—which most production applications are—this trade-off is highly favorable for responsiveness.
3.  **Consistency:** By updating the index within the `addStudent` method, we ensure the index remains "in sync" with the primary data source, preventing stale query results.

