I'll implement a solution that demonstrates how adding a secondary index improves query performance in a database context. Since this is a technical interview, I'll show both the concept and a practical implementation.

## Approach

We'll use an in-memory database example with a `users` table that has a primary key on `id`. We want to query by `email` frequently, so we'll add a secondary index on that column. The implementation will:
1. Show the table structure with and without the index
2. Benchmark query performance before and after adding the index
3. Use a hash-based index for O(1) lookups vs full table scans

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SecondaryIndexExample {

    // Database tables represented as in-memory structures
    static class User {
        int id;
        String email;
        String name;

        User(int id, String email, String name) {
            this.id = id;
            this.email = email;
            this.name = name;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return String.format("User{id=%d, email='%s', name='%s'}", id, email, name);
        }
    }

    static class UserDatabase {
        // Primary storage - keyed by id (primary key)
        private final Map<Integer, User> primaryStore = new HashMap<>();

        // Secondary index on email - null means no index
        private Map<String, Integer> emailIndex = null;

        // For measuring table scan cost
        private long totalRows = 0;

        void insertUser(int id, String email, String name) {
            User user = new User(id, email, name);
            primaryStore.put(id, user);
            totalRows++;

            // Keep secondary index in sync
            if (emailIndex != null) {
                emailIndex.put(email, id);
            }
        }

        void createEmailIndex() {
            System.out.println("Creating secondary index on email...");
            long start = System.nanoTime();
            emailIndex = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

            // Simulate building index by scanning all rows
            for (User user : primaryStore.values()) {
                emailIndex.put(user.email, user.id);
            }
            long end = System.nanoTime();
            System.out.printf("Index created in %.3f ms%n", (end - start) / 1_000_000.0);
        }

        // Query without index - full table scan O(n)
        User findUserByEmailWithoutIndex(String email) {
            long start = System.nanoTime();

            Optional<User> result = primaryStore.values().stream()
                    .filter(u -> u.email.equals(email))
                    .findFirst();

            long end = System.nanoTime();
            System.out.printf("Full table scan: %.3f ms | ", (end - start) / 1_000_000.0);

            return result.orElse(null);
        }

        // Query with secondary index - O(1) lookup
        User findUserByEmailWithIndex(String email) {
            if (emailIndex == null) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Secondary index not created");
            }

            long start = System.nanoTime();
            Integer id = emailIndex.get(email);
            User user = (id != null) ? primaryStore.get(id) : null;
            long end = System.nanoTime();

            System.out.printf("Index lookup: %.3f ms | ", (end - start) / 1_000_000.0);
            return user;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UserDatabase db = new UserDatabase();

        // Populate database with 100,000 users
        System.out.println("Populating database with 100,000 users...");
        Random rand = new Random(42);
        Set<String> usedEmails = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = 1; i <= 100_000; i++) {
            String email;
            do {
                email = "user" + i + "@example.com";
            } while (!usedEmails.add(email));
            db.insertUser(i, email, "User " + i);
        }
        System.out.println("Database populated.\n");

        // Test query without index
        System.out.println("=== Testing query WITHOUT index ===");
        System.out.println("Query 1: Searching for user99999@example.com");
        long totalTimeWithoutIndex = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {  // Run 100 queries to get average
            totalTimeWithoutIndex = totalTimeWithoutIndex * 0;  // reset method
        }

        // Do proper timing
        long avgWithoutIndex = benchmarkQueries(db, false, 100);

        // Create index
        db.createEmailIndex();

        // Test query with index
        System.out.println("\n=== Testing query WITH index ===");
        long avgWithIndex = benchmarkQueries(db, true, 100);

        // Results
        System.out.println("\n=== Results ===");
        System.out.printf("Average query time WITHOUT index: %.6f ms%n", avgWithoutIndex);
        System.out.printf("Average query time WITH index:    %.6f ms%n", avgWithIndex);
        System.out.printf("Speedup: %.2fx%n", avgWithoutIndex / avgWithIndex);
    }

    private static long benchmarkQueries(UserDatabase db, boolean useIndex, int numQueries) {
        long totalTime = 0;
        Random rand = new Random(123);

        for (int i = 0; i < numQueries; i++) {
            int targetId = 1 + rand.nextInt(100_000);
            String email = "user" + targetId + "@example.com";

            long start = System.nanoTime();
            if (useIndex) {
                db.findUserByEmailWithIndex(email);
            } else {
                db.findUserByEmailWithoutIndex(email);
            }
            long end = System.nanoTime();
            totalTime += (end - start);
        }

        return totalTime / (numQueries * 1_000_000.0); // milliseconds
    }
}
```

## Explanation of Why This Works

1. **Without secondary index**: The database must scan every record (O(n) complexity) to find matching emails. For 100,000 users, this means checking all 100,000 records on average 50,000 times for each query.

2. **With secondary index**: We create a balanced hash map (HashMap) that maps email → primary key id. Now queries become O(1) - direct hash lookup, then a quick primary key access.

3. **Index maintenance**: When inserting new records, we maintain the index incrementally (shown in `insertUser` method), so there's no need to rebuild it for every query.

4. **Real database analogy**: This is exactly what relational databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL) do internally. The secondary index is a separate data structure (typically B-tree or hash) that stores column values → row locations, dramatically speeding up WHERE clause lookups.

The performance improvement scales with data size - for very large tables with millions of rows, the difference becomes even more dramatic (milliseconds vs nanoseconds).

